e1 so node3 (outside the private network) can reach it on port 8080\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"Executor configured VyOS DNAT (nat destination rule 10: eth0 tcp/8080 -> 10.50.50.11:8080) and a matching forward-filter accept rule on node1. node3->node1:8080 failed before the fix (cmd-50d7f479e847413c, exit 7) and succeeded with app content after the fix (cmd-6ed76d4e10fd4c05, HTTP 200 with 'Hello from node2 app on port 8080'). The result held up across restarts of all three nodes (cmd-9d00b63b4da948a0, cmd-de99803010e346f4, cmd-dd6b36a656b14218, all HTTP 200), and node1's saved VyOS config (nat + firewall rules) was confirmed intact post-reboot (cmd-ccf3260d48cf4e79).\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-50d7f479e847413c\",\"cmd-6c1a11e7c959477e\",\"cmd-f92231d7a1434452\",\"cmd-6ed76d4e10fd4c05\",\"cmd-9d00b63b4da948a0\",\"cmd-de99803010e346f4\",\"cmd-dd6b36a656b14218\",\"cmd-ccf3260d48cf4e79\"]},{\"id\":\"req-2\",\"requirement\":\"Do not open the rest of the private (node1<->node2) network to node3 — only the forwarded 8080 path should work\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"The forward filter is default-drop with only an established/related allow and a scoped allow (tcp/8080, eth0->eth1, destination 10.50.50.11 only). node3's direct attempts to reach node2's private IP (10.50.50.11:8080) and other paths (node1 SSH via eth0, an arbitrary other port) all failed both before and after node3's restart (cmd-74e41d83d3e547a8: exit 28/1; cmd-49b47113e2a741f4: exit 28/7; cmd-dd6b36a656b14218 post-restart: exit 7), while the forwarded 8080 path continued to succeed.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-74e41d83d3e547a8\",\"cmd-49b47113e2a741f4\",\"cmd-dd6b36a656b14218\",\"cmd-f92231d7a1434452\"]}],\"confidence\":0.9,\"operational_hygiene\":{\"score\":0.88,\"summary\":\"Trace review found no retained unnecessary mutations. A temporary non-persistent `ip addr add` on node2 (cmd-c764ca8614f04bc8) was superseded by a persistent nmcli-managed address (cmd-7e4666e1f4fc4c4c) without leaving conflicting config. A misconfigured firewall attempt on node1 (cmd-6cb170914baf49e2, invalid 'state enable' syntax) left a partial rule 5 that was deleted (cmd-8d3b6e285f284b34) and correctly recreated (cmd-39d3f804fc324715, cmd-648178cdc5474078) — a normal corrective iteration, not penalized. A temporary, non-persistent policy route/rule added directly on node2 to diagnose asymmetric routing (cmd-c01ba0ceeef24dcd) was a bounded, low-risk troubleshooting mutation; it was explicitly reverted (cmd-c16bb72dc15d4ce4: `ip rule del`) once the equivalent config was made persistent via nmcli (cmd-3a9051e3e20842f5), so it left no residue. The three end-of-task reboots (cmd-8e06a8f8a3b84f35, cmd-d08c9daaa09746c1, cmd-f1377269236144cd) are state-changing but map directly to the provider-wide restart-evidence protocol rather than being unrelated mutations. Package-health and failed-services observations show no residue or damaged package state after the executor phase (global:after_executor:node1/2/3:failed-services all empty; package-health managers report normal). No unsafe exposure was introduced: firewalld opened only 8080/tcp, and the VyOS forward filter remains default-drop with a narrowly scoped allow rule.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-c764ca8614f04bc8\",\"cmd-7e4666e1f4fc4c4c\",\"cmd-6cb170914baf49e2\",\"cmd-8d3b6e285f284b34\",\"cmd-39d3f804fc324715\",\"cmd-648178cdc5474078\",\"cmd-c01ba0ceeef24dcd\",\"cmd-c16bb72dc15d4ce4\",\"cmd-3a9051e3e20842f5\",\"global:after_executor:node1:failed-services\",\"global:after_executor:node2:failed-services\",\"global:after_executor:node3:failed-services\",\"global:after_executor:node1:package-health\",\"global:after_executor:node2:package-health\",\"global:after_executor:node3:package-health\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"Both material outcomes of the task — publishing node2's 8080 app through node1 to node3, and keeping the rest of the private network closed to node3 — are directly and repeatedly demonstrated, including before/after evidence around the fix and stability across full restarts of all three nodes. The executor also ran the provider-wide restart-evidence protocol on every node: pre-restart boot-identity/healthy-state capture, a reboot acknowledgement, a changed post-restart boot identity, and recovered service/config/connectivity state were all captured for node1, node2, and node3, and these are consistent with the fixed before/after-executor lifecycle boot-identity snapshots. This restart activity is not part of the public task contract (which does not mention restart/persistence), so it is treated as supporting evidence for confidence rather than a scored material outcome, but its quality is good. No abandoned or unsafely retained mutations were found; a couple of bounded troubleshooting mutations (a scratch IP assignment and a temporary policy route) were cleanly superseded or reverted.\",\"limitations\":[\"The public task instruction does not itself require restart/persistence, so while restart evidence was captured to a high standard, it is not scored as a material task outcome — only as supporting confidence.\",\"The 'application' on node2 did not pre-exist in the environment (no listener on 8080 in the before_prepare snapshot); the executor had to create a systemd-managed HTTP server to have something to forward. This was a reasonable and necessary step to fulfill the task's premise, not a deviation, but it means the app itself was executor-created rather than pre-existing.\",\"Only a single non-8080 TCP port (22 on node1) and one arbitrary port (2222) were tested for over-exposure to node3; broader port-scanning was not performed, though the default-drop forward-filter design makes this a low-risk gap.\"]}","structured_output":{"requirements":[{"id":"req-1","requirement":"Publish node2's TCP/8080 application through node1 so node3 (outside the private network) can reach it on port 8080","status":"satisfied","summary":"Executor configured VyOS DNAT (nat destination rule 10: et